PHASE 1: SYSTEM ANALYSIS

1. Introduction

The JKUAT Information System is a proposed system designed to help manage information concerning students, lecturers and non-teaching staff.

The system will connect the Student Portal and Learning Management System (LMS) so that important academic information can be shared between the two systems.

 2. Problem Statement

The university requires an efficient way of managing student and lecturer information. Students need to register units through the official Student Portal and access the same units through the LMS.

Lecturers also need to upload learning materials, create assignments and CATs, and enter student marks. The marks entered by lecturers should be available to students through the Student Portal.

Without proper coordination between the systems, information may be duplicated, delayed or inconsistent.

 3. Main Objective

To develop an information system that manages students, lecturers and academic information while improving communication between the Student Portal and LMS.

4. Specific Objectives

- To manage student information.
- To manage lecturer information.
- To allow students to register units.
- To make registered units available on the LMS.
- To allow lecturers to upload notes and create assessments.
- To allow lecturers to enter student marks.
- To make marks entered in the LMS available on the Student Portal.
- To improve the management of academic information.

5. System Users

The main users of the system are:

Students
Students will be able to:
- Log into the Student Portal.
- Register units.
- View registered units.
- Access the LMS.
- View marks and grades.

Lecturers
Lecturers will be able to:
- Log into the system.
- View assigned units.
- Upload notes.
- Create assignments.
- Create CATs.
- Enter student marks.

Non-Teaching Staff

Non-teaching staff will assist in managing administrative information and supporting university operations.

6. Functional Requirements

The system should be able to:

1. Register and manage student accounts.
2. Register and manage lecturer accounts.
3. Allow students to register units.
4. Display registered units on the LMS.
5. Allow lecturers to manage learning materials.
6. Allow lecturers to enter student marks.
7. Display student marks through the Student Portal.
8. Allow users to log in securely.
9. Display student grades based on marks.

7. Non-Functional Requirements

Security
Only authorised users should access the system.

 Reliability
The system should provide accurate academic information.

Usability
The system should be simple and easy to use.

 Performance
The system should respond to user requests within a reasonable time.

### Maintainability
The system should be easy to update and maintain.

 8. Feasibility Study

 Technical Feasibility

The system can be developed using programming technologies such as C++. The project can initially be developed as a prototype and later connected to a database.

Economic Feasibility

The proposed system can reduce manual work and duplication of information. Open-source development tools can also be used to reduce development costs.

Operational Feasibility

The system is practical because students and lecturers already use university academic systems. The proposed system will improve coordination between these systems.

9. Proposed System

The proposed system will connect the Student Portal and LMS.

The basic process will be:

Student registers a unit  
↓  
Unit is stored in the system  
↓  
Registered unit becomes available on LMS  
↓  
Lecturer teaches the unit  
↓  
Lecturer enters student marks  
↓  
Marks are stored  
↓  
Student views marks through Student Portal

10. Expected Benefits

- Improved management of student information.
- Better coordination between the Student Portal and LMS.
- Reduced duplication of academic information.
- Faster access to marks and grades.
- Easier management of learning materials.
- Improved communication between students and lecturers.

 11. Conclusion

The proposed JKUAT Information System will provide a centralised way of managing important student and lecturer information. By coordinating the Student Portal and LMS, the system will improve the flow and consistency of academic information.
